+Vision-language-action (VLA) policies map language instructions and visual observations to robot actions, providing a general framework for language-conditioned manipulation. 
+Diffusion and flow-matching action generators have been widely adopted in VLA policies to model continuous action trajectories for robot control~\cite{chi2025diffusionpolicy,wen2025diffusionvla,black2025pi0}.
+However, their performance is constrained by the visual evidence available from the camera view at deployment. 
+When object pose, spatial relations, or gripper-object contact are occluded or only weakly visible, a policy relying on that view can perform substantially worse than one with access to a more informative view.
 On LIBERO, policies trained and evaluated separately for each camera view exhibit substantial differences in task success (Figure~\ref{fig:motivation}).
-Workspace geometry and robot hardware can constrain camera placement, leaving a less informative deployment view.
-This motivates weak-view policy learning, where a policy uses a limited view at deployment but can receive training-time supervision from a more informative view.
+In practical systems, workspace geometry and robot hardware can constrain camera placement, leaving the policy with a less informative deployment view. 
+This practical constraint motivates weak-view policy learning, where a policy is deployed with a limited camera view but can receive training-time supervision from a more informative view.
 
-View-diversity methods address viewpoint sensitivity using synchronized multi-camera demonstrations or synthesized novel views~\cite{ablett2021multiview,tian2025vista}. Auxiliary views also support visual representation learning~\cite{seo2023multiview}, while geometry-aware methods encode scenes using explicit 3D representations~\cite{shridhar2023peract,goyal2023rvt}. These strategies improve viewpoint robustness but do not explicitly identify the visual evidence needed for each action prediction under weak-view deployment.
+Prior work has addressed viewpoint sensitivity by showing the policy more camera views during training or by preserving task-relevant scene structure across viewpoints. View-diversity methods train on synchronized multi-camera demonstrations or synthesized novel views~\cite{ablett2021multiview,tian2025vista}. Auxiliary views also support visual representation learning~\cite{seo2023multiview}, while geometry-aware methods encode scenes using explicit 3D representations~\cite{shridhar2023peract,goyal2023rvt}. These strategies improve robustness to camera changes, but they do not explicitly identify which visual evidence matters to each action prediction under weak-view deployment.
 
-Privileged distillation supervises a weak-view policy using a teacher with more informative views during training. Prior work transfers knowledge from a multi-camera teacher to a single-camera policy by matching teacher actions and visual features~\cite{acar2024multicamera}. However, cross-view supervision faces a correspondence problem: relevant evidence may be occluded in the weak view, while shared scene elements can appear at different image locations and token positions. Consequently, matching complete representations across views can impose poorly aligned targets on the student. Effective cross-view supervision must identify teacher information useful for each future action, since different actions may require different visual evidence.
+Privileged distillation offers a more targeted alternative by supervising the weak-view policy with a teacher that observes a more informative view during training. Prior work transfers knowledge from a multi-camera teacher to a single-camera policy by matching teacher actions and visual features~\cite{acar2024multicamera}. In the cross-view setting, however, this supervision faces a correspondence problem. Relevant evidence may be occluded in the weak view, while shared scene elements can appear at different image locations and token positions. Consequently, matching complete representations from informative and weak views can impose poorly aligned targets on the weak-view policy. Effective cross-view supervision must identify teacher information useful for action prediction despite this misalignment. It must also adapt across the action horizon because different future actions may depend on different visual evidence.
 
 To address these challenges, we propose \textbf{Action-Conditioned Privileged Distillation (ACPD)}, a cross-view distillation framework that transfers action-conditioned visual cues from a privileged-view teacher to a weak-view student.
-ACPD trains the student alongside a frozen teacher using synchronized observations with the same instruction, robot state, and action target. The teacher receives informative camera views, while the student receives a single weak view. For each future control step, ACPD uses attention weights from the teacher's action expert to aggregate visual features from each privileged view into a compact cue. The student learns to predict these cues from its own action representations and incorporates them into action generation. These cues provide supervision without requiring visual-token alignment across views.
-At the action level, ACPD introduces an \textit{action correlation loss} (ACL) that uses Pearson correlation to align the relative variation of teacher and student flow-velocity predictions. The ground-truth flow-matching objective supervises the student's absolute velocity values. At deployment, the student uses a single camera view without the teacher.
+ACPD trains the student alongside a frozen teacher using synchronized observations with the same instruction, robot state, and action target. The teacher receives informative camera views, while the student receives a single weak view. For each future control step, ACPD uses attention weights from the teacher's action expert to aggregate visual features from each privileged view into a compact cue. The student learns to predict these cues from its own action representations and incorporates them into action generation. This provides supervision for individual steps in the action sequence without requiring visual-token alignment across camera views.
+At the action level, ACPD introduces an \textit{action correlation loss} (ACL) that uses Pearson correlation to align the relative variation of teacher and student flow-velocity predictions. The ground-truth flow-matching objective supervises the student's absolute velocity values. Together, privileged-cue prediction and ACL provide supervision at the representation and action levels. At deployment, the student predicts and uses these cues from a single camera view, independently of the teacher.
 
 We evaluate ACPD with $\pi_{0.5}$ across four LIBERO suites and four single-view deployment settings. ACPD improves mean success over single-view fine-tuning in all four views, with gains of 2.90--4.00 percentage points and an overall increase from 71.09\% to 74.78\%. In the back-view setting, ACPD achieves 61.05\% success, exceeding the evaluated multi-view-to-single-view distillation baseline by 8.65 percentage points.
 
